  [1mC. Event bus: [38;5;153mlogin[39m emits, a tracker subscribes[0m

  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m dispatches a [38;5;153mlogin:success[39m event; a tracker module listens, stores, and logs. Fully decoupled; other forms
  subscribe to their own events later.

  - [1mBuys:[0m genuinely extensible if tracking grows to many events from many places.
  - [1mCosts:[0m an indirection layer on a 25-line app, and the flow becomes non-obvious to read. This is the YAGNI casualty —
    I'd only pick it if you already know several other forms will emit tracking events.

  [1mWhy A:[0m it gives you the shared, persisted, reusable piece you asked for, keeps the seam between "authenticate" and
  "remember who" visible, and is the cheapest of the three to change when the real API call replaces the stub.

  [1mTwo things I'd fold into A, which I'll detail in the design if you pick it:[0m

  1. [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m is still a stub[0m — it returns a hardcoded object and never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. A real call is async, which
     would make [38;5;153mlogin()[39m return a Promise and change the call site again. I'd keep it synchronous now and have the stub
     return a clearly-marked placeholder [38;5;153muserId[39m, rather than half-building an async path against an API that isn't
     wired.
  2. [1mThere's no test infrastructure at all[0m — no runner, no scripts, no dependencies in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. A storage module
     with absent/malformed-value handling is exactly the kind of thing worth a test, so I'll ask whether to set one up
     as part of this.
